::GEO_U06_L07_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.11
Question:
A plane slices a cylinder parallel to its circular bases. What cross-section is formed?
{
=A. Circle#A slice parallel to the circular bases has the same circular shape.
~B. Triangle#A cylinder does not form a triangle from a base-parallel slice.
~C. Rectangle#A perpendicular slice may form a rectangle, but not this slice.
~D. Parabola#A parabola is a conic section, not this cylinder cross-section.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.12
Question:
Rotating a rectangle around one side creates which solid?
{
~A. Cone#A cone comes from rotating a right triangle around a leg.
=B. Cylinder#A rectangle rotated around one side sweeps out a cylinder.
~C. Sphere#A sphere can come from rotating a semicircle.
~D. Pyramid#A pyramid is not created by rotating a rectangle around one side.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.16
Question:
Which conic section can be formed by slicing a cone parallel to its base?
{
~A. Parabola#A parabola comes from a different angled slice.
~B. Ellipse only#A base-parallel slice is specifically circular.
=C. Circle#A slice parallel to the cone base forms a circle.
~D. Line segment only#A cone slice can form curves, not only a line segment.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.11
Question:
Which statement best supports a cross-section conclusion?
{
~A. The solid looks wide#Appearance alone is not enough evidence.
~B. The diagram is shaded#Shading may help visualize but does not identify the slice.
~C. The answer is always a circle#Different solids and slicing directions create different shapes.
=D. The shape depends on the solid and the direction of the slice#Cross-sections are determined by both the solid and slicing plane.
}

::GEO_U06_L07_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L07_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.CGM.12
Question:
Rotating a semicircle around its diameter creates which object?
{
=A. Sphere#A semicircle rotated around its diameter sweeps out a sphere.
~B. Cylinder#A cylinder comes from rotating a rectangle.
~C. Cone#A cone comes from rotating a right triangle.
~D. Prism#A prism is not created by this rotation.
}

